{% from "macros.njk" import callout, snippet with context %}

{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>A bottom sheet presents choices or a compact task in the context the user
     is already in. Material 2 defines three types; Flint ships two — the two
     that earn their keep on the web.</p>
  <h3>Modal sheet</h3>
  <p>Blocks the screen behind a scrim and dismisses as soon as the choice is
     made. Use it for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Choosing from a set</strong> — sort order, account picker,
        "add to" destinations. The canonical sheet is a vertical option
        list.</li>
    <li><strong>Compact forms</strong> — "new label", quick compose, a filter
        with two or three controls.</li>
    <li><strong>Details that shouldn't navigate</strong> — long metadata or an
        "about" panel that belongs to the current screen.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Standard sheet</h3>
  <p>Persistent and non-modal: no scrim, the page stays interactive and
     scrolls behind it. Use it for a state the app keeps — a mini-player, a
     cart summary, a "3 items selected" bar. It is plain CSS (a fixed block
     element); there is nothing to open and nothing to dismiss.</p>
  <h3>Don't use a sheet for</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Destructive or blocking decisions</strong> — "delete
        everything?" is <a href="dialog.html">dialog</a> territory; a sheet
        implies "this is still the same task".</li>
    <li><strong>Full-screen forms</strong> — real compose and settings
        sub-pages are the dialog's <code>--fullscreen</code> type.</li>
    <li><strong>Feedback</strong> — "Message sent" is a snackbar, never a
        sheet.</li>
    <li><strong>An "expanded" state of a collapsed pill</strong> — that is
        M2's expanding sheet, which Flint deliberately does not implement:
        on the web its collapsed state is a FAB or a sticky action bar and
        its expanded state is the dialog's <code>--fullscreen</code>. The
        only thing left would be the morph between them — a gesture
        state machine this library doesn't need.</li>
  </ul>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "No drag gesture",
    text: "M2 sheets have no drag handle, and Flint doesn't fake one — there is no swipe-to-dismiss either: a vertical drag fights the sheet's own scroll. Sheets close on scrim tap, Escape, their own rows or a close action.",
    href: "dialog.html",
    linkText: "Dialog component"
  }) }}
</section>

