        <g><circle cx="300" cy="180" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="300" y="184" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">2</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="300" cy="240" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="300" y="244" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">3</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="565" cy="115" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="565" y="119" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">4</text></g>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Title</strong> — short, 16px/500; optional for pure option
          lists where the rows are self-describing.</li>
      <li><strong>Content slot</strong> — exactly one of <code>__list</code>,
          <code>__body</code> or <code>__grid</code>; it scrolls while title
          and actions stay pinned. The surface has no padding — each slot
          owns its inset.</li>
      <li><strong>Actions</strong> — right-aligned, same button idiom as the
          dialog; often unnecessary for pure pick-one lists. They carry the
          safe-area inset.</li>
      <li><strong>Scrim</strong> — modal only, 32% black, shared with the
          dialog and drawer. A standard sheet has none.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-behavior">Behavior</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Two roots, one contract:</strong> the modal sheet is a
        <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> (only dialogs can be modal); the standard
        sheet is any block element with <code>fl-sheet--standard</code>. The
        interior — surface, title, one content slot, actions — is identical,
        so the CSS is written once.</li>
    <li><strong>Modal lifecycle:</strong>
        <code>data-fl-open="sheet-id"</code> opens it,
        <code>data-fl-close</code> inside closes it, and
        <code>&lt;form method="dialog"&gt;</code> records the pressed row or
        button in <code>dialog.returnValue</code> — picking "Unread first"
        returns <code>"unread"</code> with zero JavaScript.</li>
    <li><strong>Height:</strong> the initial height is capped at
        <code>50dvh</code> — M2's "initial position at 50% of the screen".
        Shorter content sizes the sheet; taller content scrolls inside it.
        Content that needs the whole screen gets <code>fl-sheet--full</code>
        (100dvh, internal scroll) — and a close action is <em>required</em>
        in that state, because the scrim can't be the only way out of a
        full-screen surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Dismissal (modal):</strong> Escape, scrim tap, the rows and
        the actions all close it; focus returns to the invoker on every
        path. There is no swipe-to-dismiss — a vertical drag has to
        arbitrate against the sheet's own scroll, and a gesture that
        sometimes scrolls and sometimes closes is worse than none.</li>
    <li><strong>Scroll:</strong> the content slot scrolls inside the surface
        (<code>overscroll-behavior: contain</code> keeps the page behind
        from rubber-banding); the page itself is scroll-locked while a
        modal sheet is open — one CSS rule shared across dialog, sheet and
        drawer.</li>
    <li><strong>Stacking:</strong> a <a href="dialog.html">dialog</a> can open
        over the sheet — the top layer stacks in DOM order and Escape peels
        only the top one.</li>
    <li><strong>Animation:</strong> slides up on open, fades out on close —
        CSS only, <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> disables it.</li>
    <li><strong>No-JS fallback (honest):</strong> the standard sheet is plain
        CSS and works without JavaScript. The modal sheet stays hidden —
        call <code>showModal()</code> yourself with one line, or drive it
        from your own code. See <a href="../demos/dialog.html">demos/dialog.html</a>
        for the raw lifecycle.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-placement">Placement</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mobile (&lt;600px):</strong> full-bleed to the bottom edge,
        8px top radius. On the web the browser owns the edges, so the
        sheet docks to the layout viewport, not behind the URL bar.</li>
    <li><strong>Desktop (≥600px):</strong> a 560px card docked to the bottom
        edge, with a taller cap (<code>min(86dvh, 640px)</code>). Never
        full-width on large screens — an edge-to-edge tray at desktop width
        is a layout, not a sheet.</li>
    <li><strong>Safe areas:</strong> the actions row and the standard sheet
        add <code>env(safe-area-inset-bottom)</code>, so the home indicator
        never covers a button.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't inset a sheet on all sides</strong> — that's a card or
        (small and inverse) a snackbar, not a sheet.</li>
    <li><strong>Standard sheet layering:</strong> it sits below the snackbar
        (<code>--fl-z-sheet: 18</code> vs <code>--fl-z-snackbar: 25</code>)
        so status messages always win the bottom edge. The page needs
        <code>padding-bottom</code> clearance at least as tall as the
        sheet.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-contents">Contents</h2>
  <p>One content slot per sheet — no nesting, no negative margins:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>__list</code></strong> — option rows, full-bleed.
        Rows are <code>fl-option</code> at <strong>56px</strong> here (the
        surface sets <code>--fl-option-h</code>; dialogs keep 48px — both
        are spec numbers, one row component).</li>
    <li><strong><code>__grid</code></strong> — the "Open in…" idiom: a fixed
        3-column grid of icon+label tiles. The tiles are the same
        <code>fl-option</code> re-oriented vertically.</li>
    <li><strong><code>__body</code></strong> — fields and prose; owns the
        side insets and scrolls.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Give the sheet <code>aria-labelledby</code> pointing at its title
        (skip this only when there is deliberately no title and the rows are
        self-describing).</li>
    <li>Option rows are real <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>s or
        <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>+input pairs — native focus, native
        activation, no <code>tabindex</code> games. 56px rows exceed the
        48px touch-target minimum.</li>
    <li>The inert background is native: nothing behind a modal sheet is
        focusable or announced while it's open. The standard sheet is
        <em>not</em> inert — keep its controls few and its content a state,
        not a task.</li>
    <li>Full-bleed rows draw the focus ring inside the row (the global ring
        would land outside the surface and get clipped) — don't remove it;
        it's what makes keyboard position visible.</li>
    <li><strong>Known gap:</strong> no swipe-to-dismiss (see Behavior) and
        no Android back-gesture integration — on Android the system back
        navigates away instead of closing the sheet. All four web dismissal
        paths work; a history-based fix is planned for 0.5.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endset %}

